    <h1>About</h1>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">
  <section class="block" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <p>VCLV is a one-person engineering design studio built on a simple habit: taking things apart to understand how they actually work, then rebuilding them better. That habit runs through every project on this site, whether it's reconstructing a decades-old glass-grinding process from a video recording, converting a desktop 3D printer to industrial-grade firmware, or writing a Python layer that lets a robot speak a slicer's language.</p>
    <p>The work sits at the intersection of mechanical engineering, industrial design, and digital manufacturing — moving between CAD and Blender, between G-code and robot programs, between a part on a screen and a part in your hand. Most projects start the same way: a process that already exists somewhere, done by hand or on older equipment, gets studied closely enough to be reproduced, automated, or pushed further with robotics and additive manufacturing.</p>
    <p>That range shows up in the tools below — from cast-iron lens grinding to robotic large-format printing to firmware-level printer control — less a fixed specialty than a working method applied to whatever the project needs.</p>
    <p>VCLV products, when they arrive, will follow the same instinct applied to optics: light, polariscopy, and lenticular effects, built with the same digital manufacturing tools used throughout this work.</p>
  </section>

